# q6_sim 250 2483.2
Task 6, stage 5  two designs simulated over 600000 play requests

inputs
  SSD random read     : 0.250 ms (conservative device latency; the
        page-cache hits measured on this machine were about 0.005 ms)
  SSD sequential read : 2483 MB/s (measured)
  hard disk           : 8.5 ms seek + 0.07 ms queue + 200 MB/s
  CDN edge / origin   : 15 ms / +120 ms, client at 6.25 MB/s
  internet path for the company's own servers: +25 ms
  buffer to fill before playback: 1.25 MB at 6.25 MB/s = 200 ms
  (the same client cost for both designs)

design                       |    mean |     p50 |     p95 | p99
-----------------------------+---------+---------+---------+----------
A own, student on campus     |   201.9 |   200.2 |   208.6 |   208.6 ms
A own, student at home       |   226.9 |   225.2 |   233.6 |   233.6 ms
B CDN, student on campus     |   221.0 |   215.0 |   215.0 |   335.0 ms
B CDN, student at home       |   221.0 |   215.0 |   215.0 |   335.0 ms

mean of the two populations (30% at home)
  A own servers : 209.4 ms
  B CDN         : 221.0 ms

monthly cost of the two designs (1.6 PB of viewing)
  A own: 6.4 TB of disks (128 USD) + 1600000 GB of transit at 0.01 USD = 16000 USD
  B CDN: 3.2 TB of object storage (74 USD) + 1600000 GB at 0.02 USD = 32000 USD

reading the result: on campus the company's own SSD cache starts
playback 15 ms sooner than the CDN; for the students at home the
company's servers lose their advantage to the internet path and the
CDN is faster.  The CDN also removes the 50 Gbps peak from the
company's uplink, which is the constraint no cache can fix.
The measured recommendation is therefore a hybrid: the company's
servers keep the catalogue and serve campus, and a CDN in front of
them serves the students at home and absorbs the peak.
